Britain's iconic red squirrel is in  trouble the problem the invasive eastern  gray squirrel  brought to England from North America  about a hundred and forty years ago  wealthy collectors in the 19th century  like to keep gray squirrels as pets  or release them onto their estates as  kind of a living lawn ornament in fact  there's genetic evidence that all of the  grey squirrels and ions are descended  from six pairs sent as a wedding gift  the grey squirrel is bad news for the  native red squirrels because they  out-compete them for food and habitat  worst of all grave carry a virus called  squirrel pox it doesn't harm the grey  squirrels or people for that matter but  it is deadly to the race so many  conservation groups working to save the  red squirrel have had some successes  grey squirrels have been eradicated from  the Isle of Anglesey it took eighteen  years but now the islands red squirrels  are on the rebound and there is hope  that nature may help the pine marten a  relative of weasels is a native predator  scientists in Ireland have noticed an  intriguing pattern where pine martens  show up to the population of grey  squirrels declines and red squirrels  start to recover it may be that red  squirrels which evolved with the pine  marten are better able to escape these  tree climbing predators the future of  the red squirrel is looking brighter in  the UK but there's still much to be done  the lesson for other countries is to  prevent gray squirrels from arriving at  all or getting rid of them as quickly as  possible and to definitely not give them  as wedding presents
 

